York University, New York, United States."}]}}, {"fullName": "Winawer J", "lastName": "Winawer", "firstName": "Jonathan", "initials": "J", "authorId": {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-7475-5586"}, "authorAffiliationDetailsList": {"authorAffiliation": [{"affiliation": "Department of Psychology, New York University, New York, United States."}, {"affiliation": "Center for Neural Sciences, New York University, New York, United States."}]}}]}, "authorIdList": {"authorId": [{"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0001-7475-5586"}, {"type": "ORCID", "value": "0000-0002-2365-8265"}]}, "dataLinksTagsList": {"dataLinkstag": ["altmetrics", "supporting_data"]}, "journalInfo": {"volume": "7", "journalIssueId": 2755883, "dateOfPublication": "2018 Dec", "monthOfPublication": 12, "yearOfPublication": 2018, "journal": {"medlineAbbreviation": "Elife", "title": "eLife", "essn": "2050-084X", "issn": "2050-084X", "nlmid": "101579614", "isoabbreviation": "Elife"}, "printPublicationDate": "2018-12-01"}, "pubYear": "2018", "pageInfo": "e40224", "abstractText": "Human visual cortex is organized into multiple retinotopic maps. Characterizing the arrangement of these maps on the cortical surface is essential to many visual neuroscience studies. Typically, maps are obtained by voxel-wise analysis of fMRI data. This method, while useful, maps only a portion of the visual field and is limited by measurement noise and subjective assessment of boundaries. We developed a novel Bayesian mapping approach which combines observation-a subject's retinotopic measurements from small amounts of fMRI time-with a prior-a learned retinotopic atlas. This process automatically draws areal boundaries, corrects discontinuities in the measured maps, and predicts validation data more accurately than an atlas alone or independent datasets alone. This new method can be used to improve the accuracy of retinotopic mapping, to analyze large fMRI datasets automatically, and to quantify differences in map properties as a function of health, development and natural variation between individuals.", "affiliation": "Department of Psychology, New York University, New York, United States.", "publicationStatus": "epublish", "language": "eng", "pubModel": "Electronic", "pubTypeList": {"pubType": ["research-article", "Journal Article", "Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]}, "grantsList": {"grant": [{"agency": "National Institute of Mental Health", "grantId": "R01 MH111417", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NEI NIH HHS", "acronym": "EY", "grantId": "P30 EY013079", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NEI NIH HHS", "acronym": "EY", "grantId": "R01 EY027401", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NEI NIH HHS", "acronym": "EY", "grantId": "R00 EY022116", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Eye Institute", "grantId": "R00-EY022116", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NEI NIH HHS", "acronym": "EY", "grantId": "R01 EY027964", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NEI NIH HHS", "acronym": "EY", "grantId": "R00-EY022116", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Eye Institute", "grantId": "R01 EY027401", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "National Eye Institute", "grantId": "R01 EY027964", "orderIn": 0}, {"agency": "NIMH NIH HHS", "acronym": "MH", "grantId": "R01 MH111417", "orderIn": 0}]}, "meshHeadingList": {"meshHeading": [{"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Visual Cortex", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "AH", "qualifierName": "anatomy & histology"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "PH", "qualifierName": "physiology"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Humans"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Magnetic Resonance Imaging"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Brain Mapping", "meshQualifierList": {"meshQualifier": [{"majorTopic_YN": "Y", "abbreviation": "MT", "qualifierName": "methods"}]}}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Bayes Theorem"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Adult"}, {"majorTopic_YN": "N", "descriptorName": "Middle Aged"}, {"majorTopic_YN